: theorem @=thm:undecidability
  Tiling $\Z^3$ by translations of a single finite connected[^connected]
  subset of $\Z^3$ is co-RE-complete,[^lean-complete] and hence undecidable.[^lean-undecidable]
  Replacing each lattice point by a unit cube,
  tiling $\R^3$ by translations of a single finite connected[^connected]
  polycube in $\R^3$ is co-RE-complete,[^lean-real] and hence undecidable.[^lean-real-undecidable]
  [^connected]:
    Connectivity of a set in $\Z^3$ is defined according
    to lattice adjacency in $\Z^3$.
    Connectivity of a polycube in $\R^3$ is thus defined according
    to face adjacency of the constituent unit cubes.
    See @sec:lean-connected.
  [^lean-complete]:
    \leanlogo{} theorem \texttt{tiling\_coRE\_complete}. See @sec:lean-z3.
  [^lean-undecidable]:
    \leanlogo{} theorem \texttt{tiling\_undecidable}. See @sec:lean-z3.
  [^lean-real]:
    \leanlogo{} theorem \texttt{real\_tiling\_coRE\_complete}. See @sec:lean-r3.
  [^lean-real-undecidable]:
    \leanlogo{} theorem \texttt{real\_tiling\_undecidable}. See @sec:lean-r3.

***Co-RE*** is the complexity class of decision problems whose "no" instances
can be recognized in finite time by an algorithm (Turing machine).
A problem is ***co-RE-hard*** if every co-RE problem can be reduced to it
via a computable many-one reduction,
and ***co-RE-complete*** if it is also in co-RE.
It is known that some co-RE-complete problems
(such as "does a given algorithm run forever?")\ are undecidable,
so co-RE-completeness implies undecidability.

# Proof Overview @=sec:overview

We prove co-RE-hardness by reducing from the problem of Wang tiling,
introduced by [[Wang]] and proved co-RE-hard by [[Berger]].
A ***Wang tile*** is a unit square with a color on each of its four edges.
Given a set $W$ of Wang tiles,
a ***Wang tiling*** assigns a tile from $W$ to each grid point in $\Z^2$
such that, when placing those tiles by translation,
touching edges have the same color.
Deciding whether a given finite set of Wang tiles admits such a tiling
is co-RE-complete [[Berger]].

Thus our main task is to construct a computable map from a
finite Wang tile set $\calT$
to a finite connected set $F_{\calT}\subset\Z^3$ such that
$\calT$ tiles $\Z^2$ if and only if
$F_{\calT}$ tiles $\Z^3$ by translations.
To achieve this, we combine five constructions,
as summarized in @fig:reduction:

: figure[tbp]
  \centering
  \begin{tikzpicture}[
    font=\small, >=Latex,
    stage/.style={draw=black!55, rounded corners=2pt, fill=black!3,
      text width=4.0cm, minimum height=1.4cm, align=center},
    arrowlabel/.style={font=\small\bfseries, fill=white, inner sep=2pt},
    reductionarrow/.style={->, semithick, draw=black!65}]
    \node[stage] (wang) at (0,0) {Wang tiling\\of $\Z^2$};
    \node[stage] (sudoku) at (5.5,0) {Sudoku array};
    \node[stage] (system) at (11,0) {Family of prototiles\\in $\Z^2\times(\Z/m\Z)$\\with a common\\set of translations};
    \node[stage] (stacked) at (0,-2.4) {Single prototile\\in $\Z^2\times(\Z/m\Z)$\\with a larger $m$};
    \node[stage] (lattice) at (5.5,-2.4) {Single prototile\\in $\Z^3$};
    \node[stage] (connected) at (11,-2.4) {Single connected prototile\\in $\Z^3$};
    \draw[reductionarrow] (wang) -- node[arrowlabel,above] {1} (sudoku);
    \draw[reductionarrow] (sudoku) -- node[arrowlabel,above] {2} (system);
    \draw[reductionarrow,rounded corners=4pt]
      (system.east) -- (13.5,0) -- (13.5,-1.2)
      -- node[arrowlabel] {3} (-2.5,-1.2)
      -- (-2.5,-2.4) -- (stacked.west);
    \draw[reductionarrow] (stacked) -- node[arrowlabel,above] {4} (lattice);
    \draw[reductionarrow] (lattice) -- node[arrowlabel,above] {5} (connected);
  \end{tikzpicture}
  \caption{The five reductions in our construction.
    Arrow numbers match the steps in the text; every reduction preserves
    existence of a solution in both directions.}
  \label{fig:reduction}

1. Greenfeld and Tao \cite[Section~5]{GTUndecidable} reduce Wang tiling
   to finding a ***Sudoku array*** --- a grid with infinitely many rows
   and a fixed number of columns (independent of the Wang tile set)
   such that every integer-slope line reads as one of a specified finite
   set of words.
   Specifically, each grid symbol specifies a Wang tile together with
   two integers; the Sudoku rule organizes these integers using powers of
   two fixed distinct primes $p,q$, whose exponents encode the two coordinates
   of the simulated plane.
   The line rules compare neighboring positions in this simulated plane
   to enforce matching edge colors.
   We additionally require that neither integer component is constant
   down any column.
2. We adapt OpenAI's cyclic tiling encoding \cite[Sections~3--5]{OpenAI2026}
   to the Sudoku array problem from the previous step.
   (OpenAI originally applied this encoding to a different construction
   of Greenfeld and Tao's \cite[Section~4]{GTUndecidable},
   which uses a one-prime Sudoku rule.)
   This adaptation is our main new work: it enforces the Wang tile constraints
   and both nonconstant-column conditions simultaneously.
   The set of translations encodes the Sudoku array, and the prototiles
   test the rules: forbidden combinations of symbols cause overlaps or gaps.
   The result is a finite family of prototiles (depending on the Wang tile set)
   that each tile a target space using the same infinite set of translations,
   if and only if the required Sudoku array exists.
   The target space being tiled is $\Z^2 \times (\Z/m\Z)$,
   a 3D integer grid whose $z$ coordinate wraps around modulo~$m$
   (where $m$ depends on the Wang tile set).
3. We apply OpenAI's stacking construction \cite[Section~6]{OpenAI2026}
   (based on an earlier construction of Greenfeld and Tao
   \cite[Theorem~3.1 and Lemma~3.2]{GTPeriodic})
   to combine the prototiles from the previous step
   into a single prototile that tiles a (larger) target space
   if and only if such a common set of translations exists.
   %The construction places each prototile in its own group of layers
   %of an auxiliary cyclic coordinate. These groups are chosen so that
   %any two shifted copies of the same group overlap, forcing each point
   %of the original target space to be covered exactly once by each
   %original prototile.
   This construction enlarges $m$ in the target space $\Z^2 \times (\Z/m\Z)$.
4. We apply the reduction of Meyerovitch, Sanadhya, and Solomon
   \cite[Theorem~1.1]{MSS}, which converts tiling problems in a quotient
   of $\Z^3$ into tiling problems in $\Z^3$,
   while preserving the number of prototiles.
   Our target space $\Z^2 \times (\Z/m\Z)$ is such a ***quotient***:
   it identifies each point $(x,y,z)$ with $(x,y,z+m)$.
   Their construction uses matching bumps and dents
   to force alignment to a scaled grid and
   to force repetition every $m$ grid steps in the $z$ direction.
   Thus we convert the prototile from the previous step
   into a single finite 3D prototile
   that tiles $\Z^3$ if and only if the original prototile
   tiles the quotient space $\Z^2 \times (\Z/m\Z)$.
5. We use a reduction of Kim \cite[Theorem~2.5]{Kim}
   which makes the prototile connected
   while preserving dimension, the number of prototiles, and tileability.
   This construction scales up the prototile, giving space to connect the
   lattice points by thin corridors, with bumps and dents to force alignment.

# Formal Verification in Lean @=sec:lean

The proof of @thm:undecidability is long and technical.
To make it checkable, we formalized it in the Lean~4 proof assistant,
building on Mathlib and on the formalization of 
Wang tiling undecidability (`lean-wang` [[LeanWang]]).
The complete Lean proof is available in [the repository](https://github.com/edemaine/translation-tiling-paper/tree/main/lean).
The key point is that a reader does not need to read the proof,
or even most of the definitions, to trust the result:
it suffices to check that the handful of definitions and theorem statements
in a single file, \texttt{[Abstract.lean](https://github.com/edemaine/translation-tiling-paper/blob/main/lean/TranslationTiling/Abstract.lean)}, accurately express the claim.
The Lean proof assistant then verifies the proofs of these statements,
and that they rely on only Lean's three standard axioms
(`propext`, `Classical.choice`, `Quot.sound`).

In the rest of this section, we explain the statements from \texttt{[Abstract.lean](https://github.com/edemaine/translation-tiling-paper/blob/main/lean/TranslationTiling/Abstract.lean)}.
(The source file \texttt{[lean/TranslationTiling/Abstract.lean](https://github.com/edemaine/translation-tiling-paper/blob/main/lean/TranslationTiling/Abstract.lean)}
remains the authoritative version.)
It imports only Mathlib and the co-RE definitions of `lean-wang`
(`CoREPred` and `CoREComplete`, defined in the same way as in the introduction),
together with a short file \texttt{[AbstractBridge.lean](https://github.com/edemaine/translation-tiling-paper/blob/main/lean/TranslationTiling/AbstractBridge.lean)} whose job is to connect
these statements to the definitions used in the rest of the formalization
(but which need not be checked by a human).

\leansnippet{Imports}

## Prototiles

A ***prototile*** is a finite list $P$ of points in $\Z^3$.
It is a list (not a set) so that it can be given as input to an algorithm;
repetitions are immaterial.

\leansnippet{Point,Prototile}

## Connectivity

Two points $a,b\in\Z^3$ are ***adjacent*** if $\|a-b\|_1=1$.
A prototile $P$ is ***connected*** if any two points in $P$
are joined by a path of adjacent points inside $P$, i.e.,
they are related by the reflexive transitive closure of
the relation "both points are in $P$ and adjacent".

\phantomsection\label{sec:lean-connected}
\leansnippet{Adjacent,Connected}

## Tiling \texorpdfstring{$\Z^3$}{Z³}

$P$ ***tiles*** $\Z^3$ if there exists a set $T\subseteq\Z^3$ of translations
such that every $x\in\Z^3$ lies in exactly one translate $t+P$ with $t\in T$,
i.e., there is exactly one $t\in T$ with $x-t\in P$.
(No periodicity or finiteness assumption is placed on $T$.)

\leansnippet{Tiles}

## Main Theorem for \texorpdfstring{$\Z^3$}{Z³}

Deciding whether a connected prototile tiles $\Z^3$ is co-RE-complete
(`LeanWang.CoREComplete`, from `lean-wang`):
the non-tilers are recursively enumerable, and every co-RE problem reduces to
it by a computable many-one reduction.
Consequently (and using only Mathlib's notion of computable predicate)
no algorithm decides it.
In both statements, the proof is a single call to a theorem in
\texttt{[AbstractBridge.lean](https://github.com/edemaine/translation-tiling-paper/blob/main/lean/TranslationTiling/AbstractBridge.lean)}.

\phantomsection\label{sec:lean-z3}
\leansnippet{tiling_coRE_complete,tiling_undecidable}

## Tiling \texorpdfstring{$\R^3$}{R³}

%As for $\Z^3$, points of $\R^3$ are functions from the three coordinate indices
%(`Fin 3`) to $\R$.
The ***solid body*** of $P$ is the union of the closed unit cubes $p+[0,1]^3$ for $p\in P$.
The prototile $P$ ***tiles $\R^3$*** if there exists a set $T\subseteq\R^3$ of
(arbitrary real) translations such that, for Lebesgue-almost every $x\in\R^3$,
there is exactly one $t\in T$ with $x-t$ in the solid body of $P$.
("Almost every" so that cubes may share boundaries.)

\leansnippet{RealPoint,Solid,RealTiles}

## Main Theorem for \texorpdfstring{$\R^3$}{R³}

Tiling $\R^3$ by translations of the solid body of a single finite connected
polycube is co-RE-complete (and hence undecidable).

\phantomsection\label{sec:lean-r3}
\leansnippet{real_tiling_coRE_complete,real_tiling_undecidable}

Together, these four Lean theorems establish @thm:undecidability.

# Our Approach to the Proof @=sec:approach

We made heavy use of AI to find, write, and formalize the proof,
and we do not claim that this paper is a complete human-readable account of it.
Instead, this paper contains only the part intended for humans:
an explanation of the problem (@sec:intro),
the main intuition behind the proof (@sec:overview),
and the precise formalization of the statement (@sec:lean).
The rigor of the proof comes from the Lean formalization rather than from the text.

A much longer LLM-generated technical article, which is not meant for
human consumption, gives the full details of the construction and its proofs.
It and the complete Lean code are available in
[the repository](https://github.com/edemaine/translation-tiling-paper)
[[TilingRepo]]
(files \texttt{[slop.texlish](https://github.com/edemaine/translation-tiling-paper/blob/main/slop.texlish)}
and \texttt{[lean/](https://github.com/edemaine/translation-tiling-paper/tree/main/lean)}, respectively).
We intend to expand this paper with further human-readable explanations
of the proof.

